# ============================================================
# TOR Hidden Service Configuration for TOR Traffic Analyzer
# ============================================================
#
# This configuration file sets up TOR to expose the 
# TOR Traffic Analyzer Streamlit app as a Hidden Service
# (Onion Service) on the TOR network.
#
# HOW TO USE:
# 1. Find your TOR configuration file (torrc):
#    - TOR Browser: TorBrowser\Browser\TorBrowser\Data\Tor\torrc
#    - Standalone TOR: C:\Users\<username>\AppData\Roaming\tor\torrc
#    - Linux: /etc/tor/torrc
#
# 2. Add the lines below to your torrc file
#
# 3. Restart TOR (or TOR Browser)
#
# 4. TOR will generate your .onion address in:
#    <HiddenServiceDir>/hostname
#
# ============================================================

# COPY BELOW THIS LINE INTO YOUR torrc:
# ============================================================

# Enable Control Port for app integration
ControlPort 9051
CookieAuthentication 1

# Hidden Service Configuration
# Windows path (create this folder first):
HiddenServiceDir C:\Users\Balaskanthan\Downloads\tamilnadu\tor_classifier\hidden_service

# Map external port 80 to local Streamlit on 8501
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:8501

# Optional: Map HTTPS port if using SSL
# HiddenServicePort 443 127.0.0.1:8501

# ============================================================
# IMPORTANT NOTES:
# ============================================================
#
# After restarting TOR, check the hostname file:
# C:\Users\Balaskanthan\Downloads\tamilnadu\tor_classifier\hidden_service\hostname
#
# This contains your unique .onion address, e.g.:
# abc123xyz456def789.onion
#
# Users can then access the analyzer at:
# http://abc123xyz456def789.onion (via TOR Browser)
#
# ============================================================
